'''Vehicle registration plates in Northern Ireland''' use a modified version of the British [[Vehicle registration plates of the United Kingdom|national registration plate system]] that was initiated for the whole of the [[United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland]] in 1903. Originally, all counties in the UK were allocated two identification letters. At that time, the whole of the island of  Ireland was in the UK, and the letters I and Z were reserved for the Irish counties. The 'I' series was used first, but by the time it came to using the 'Z' series in 1926, the [[Irish Free State]] had already come into existence, and so it was agreed that the Northern Ireland would use the AZ – YZ series, while the Free State would use the ZA – ZZ series. In 1987, the Republic of Ireland broke away from the system altogether. As of 2002 there were reportedly 794,477 recorded registration plates in [[Northern Ireland]], compared to only 50 for the island as a whole when the format was first introduced in 1903.<ref>{{cite news|url = https://www.belfasttelegraph.co.uk/news/northern-ireland/northern-ireland-vehicle-number-plates-could-be-set-for-biggest-makeover-in-45-years-38190003.html | publisher = Belfast Telegraph | newspaper = Belfasttelegraph | title = Northern Ireland vehicle number plates could be set for biggest makeover in 45 years | date = 7 June 2019 | access-date = 21 February 2020 }}</ref>

The full list of codes used in Northern Ireland appears below.

==Format==
[[File:Northern Ireland in the UK and Europe.svg|thumb|There are two number plate systems within the UK: one for Northern Ireland and another for Great Britain]]
[[File:Northern Ireland car registration plate labels-UK.svg|right|400x400px|Diagram of Northern Ireland number plate, descriptions below]]
[[File:UK (NI) Front Registration Plate.png|400x400px|This is the current NI front plate with the optional "UK" badge.]]

As in Great Britain, each code originally ran from 1 to 9999, and when one was completed, another was allocated. All possible codes had been allocated by 1957, following which reversed sequences were introduced, the first county to do so being [[County Antrim|Antrim]] in January 1958 with <code>1&nbsp;IA</code>. These reversed sequences were completed quickly, leading to the introduction of the current "<code>AXX&nbsp;1234</code>" format in January 1966, where "<code>XX</code>" is the county code and "<code>A</code>" is a serial letter. This format allowed capacity to be increased. Each county adopted it once they had completed their reversed sequences, the last one to do so being [[County Londonderry]] in October 1973 with <code>AIW&nbsp;1</code>. From November 1985, the first 100 numbers of each series were withheld for use as cherished registrations. From April 1989, the numbers 101-999 were also withheld in this way. Even multiples of 1000 and 1111 ("four-of-a-kind") are deemed cherished by the DVLA and thus withheld. Each series ends at 9998 and follows on to the next letter/number combination in the series.{{fact|date=September 2021}}
[[File:NORTHERN IRELAND, BELFAST 2000's -YELLOW REAR USE VEHICLE LICENSE PLATE - Flickr - woody1778a.jpg|thumb|Northern Ireland plate. This particular County Down plate, which dates from the last century, uses an old-style font.]]

=== International vehicle registration code on the number plates ===
The distinguishing code for the United Kingdom, including Northern Ireland is "'''UK'''".<ref name="auto"/> Prior to 28 September 2021, "'''GB'''" was used.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|title=Convention on Road Traffic Vienna, 8 November 1968: UNITED KINGDOM OF GREAT BRITAIN AND NORTHERN IRELAND: NOTIFICATION UNDER ARTICLE 45 (4)|url=https://treaties.un.org/doc/Publication/CN/2021/CN.207.2021-Eng.pdf}}</ref> Number plates featuring other codes, such as "NI" or "IRL" (the distinguishing code for Ireland) are unofficial.{{fact|date=September 2021}} From October 2021 if an owner of a vehicle wishes to avoid attaching a separate black on white “UK” sticker, it is necessary for the number plates to display “UK” on the left side. If the vehicle is driven in a country not a party to the Vienna Convention, a separate sign (black on white “UK” sticker) also has to be displayed at the rear of the vehicle.<ref>{{Cite web|title=NOTICE ON TRAVELLING BETWEEN THE EU AND THE UNITED KINGDOM FOLLOWING WITHDRAWAL OF THE UNITED KINGDOM FROM THE EU|url=https://ec.europa.eu/info/sites/info/files/travelling_en_2.pdf#page10}}</ref> Of the EU countries, a separate identifier is only needed when travelling in Cyprus, Malta, and Spain, as they are not party to the convention.<ref group="notes">"Ireland is not a party of the Vienna Convention but no separate 'GB' sticker is required." [https://www.gov.uk/guidance/driving-in-the-eu#gb-stickers-and-number-plates] Accessed in June 2021</ref>

While motorists with vehicles registered in [[Great Britain]] are permitted by the DVLA to use number plates carrying Euro-style bands with UK national flags and country codes (such as ENG, England or SCO), this is not possible in Northern Ireland.<ref name="dvlni2001">{{cite web|author=DVLNI|date=29 January 2009|title=V796(NI): Display of Registration Marks for Motor Vehicles|url=http://www.dvlni.gov.uk/vehicles/vehicle_forms/v796.pdf|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100217155358/http://www.dvlni.gov.uk/vehicles/vehicle_forms/v796.pdf|archive-date=17 February 2010|access-date=10 December 2009|df=dmy-all}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|date=7 September 2019|title=FactCheck: Will Northern Ireland drivers have to display GB stickers on their vehicles if driving in Ireland?|url=https://www.thejournal.ie/factcheck-brexit-gb-stickers-ireland-4793337-Sep2019/|access-date=21 February 2020|website=thejournal.ie|publisher=The Journal Media}}</ref> The Road Vehicles (Display of Registration Marks) (Amendment) Regulations 2009 state that ''"Paragraph (4) does not apply—(...) (b)if the relevant vehicle is recorded in the part of the register relating to Northern Ireland."'' Paragraph four reads ''"Subject to paragraphs (5) to (8), there may be displayed on a plate or other device an arrangement of letters corresponding with one of the sub-paragraphs of paragraph (9) and an emblem corresponding with one of the sub-paragraphs of paragraph (10)".''
