==== European Union symbol ====
When the UK was a member state of the European Union, it was possible to choose to display number plates conforming to the [[Vehicle registration plates of Europe#European Union|common EU format]] introduced by Council Regulation (EC) No 2411/98, with a blue strip on the left side of the plate with the [[Flag of Europe|European Union symbol]] (circle of stars) above the [[international vehicle registration code]] of the member state (GB).<ref name="euro98directive2">{{cite web|author=European Union|date=3 November 1998|title=Council Regulation (EC) No 2411/98 of 3 November 1998 on the recognition in intra-Community traffic of the distinguishing sign of the Member State in which motor vehicles and their trailers are registered|url=http://eur-lex.europa.eu/LexUriServ/LexUriServ.do?uri=CELEX:31998R2411:EN:HTML|access-date=22 October 2007}}</ref> This format can not be issued after the transition period ended.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2020/1363/made|title=The Road Vehicles (Display of Registration Marks) (Amendment) (EU Exit) (No. 2) Regulations 2020}}</ref> With Northern Ireland no longer being a part of the EU, the plates with the EU symbol are now no longer issued.{{fact|date=September 2021}}

EU member states that require foreign vehicles to display a distinguishing sign of the country of origin are obliged by Article 3 of EU Regulation No. 2411/98 to accept this standard design as a distinguishing sign when displayed on a vehicle registered in another member state, making a separate sign unnecessary for vehicles registered in the EU.{{fact|date=September 2021}}

After [[Brexit]], other EU countries are no longer required to accept UK "Europlates", as the regulation only requires member states to accept the standard design as a distinguishing sign when displayed on a vehicle registered in another member state. After this, Euro-plates must be replaced by a number plate that features the UK code (GB before 28 September 2021) in order to be valid as a national identifier.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Driving in Europe after Brexit: how leaving the EU will affect driving licences|url=https://inews.co.uk/inews-lifestyle/cars/driving-in-europe-after-brexit-how-leaving-the-eu-will-affect-uk-driving-licences-explained-1373249|access-date=3 February 2020|website=inews.co.uk|date=31 January 2020 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Displaying number plates|url=https://www.gov.uk/displaying-number-plates/flags-symbols-and-identifiers|access-date=2021-01-01|website=GOV.UK|language=en}}</ref> 
[[File:Northern Ireland Rear Registration Plate (Blank identification band).png|thumb|270x270px|A Belfast plate displaying the optional EU format "UK" country code. Any other format displaying a side badge, including those bearing the codes "NI" or "IRL", are unofficial.]]

==Administration==
The [[Administrative counties of Ireland|administrative counties of Northern Ireland]] were abolished by the [[Local Government Act (Northern Ireland) 1972]], and their responsibility for issuing registrations was transferred to the [[Minister of Home Affairs (Northern Ireland)|NI Ministry of Home Affairs]],<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.legislation.gov.uk/nisro/1973/337/contents/made|title=The Vehicles Excise (Transfer of Functions) (Supplementary Provisions) Order (Northern Ireland) 1973|work=[[Legislation.gov.uk]]|access-date=4 May 2018}}</ref> and later the [[Department of the Environment (Northern Ireland)|Department of the Environment NI]]. The former vehicle section in the [[county town]] of each local authority became a "local office" of the relevant departmental agency, formerly Driver and Vehicle Licensing Northern Ireland and latterly the [[Driver and Vehicle Agency]] (DVA) in [[Coleraine]].

From 21 July 2014, vehicle registration in Northern Ireland became the responsibility of the [[Driver and Vehicle Licensing Agency]] DVLA in [[Swansea]], which also administers the system used in Great Britain. The pre-1972 format of Northern Ireland registration plates continues unchanged in Northern Ireland.

==County codes in alphabetical order==
All the codes from <code>IA</code> to <code>IZ</code>, and from <code>AI</code> to <code>WI</code> (except for those containing G, Q, S and V, plus <code>II</code>), were [[Vehicle registration plates of the Republic of Ireland#Pre-1987 mark codes|allocated throughout the island of Ireland in 1903]], in alphabetical order of counties and then of county boroughs. Hence, Antrim was allocated <code>IA</code> and Armagh <code>IB</code>, while [[County Dublin]], now in the Republic of Ireland, was allocated <code>IK</code>, in between <code>IJ</code> for Down and <code>IL</code> for Fermanagh. Shortly before the [[partition of Ireland]] in 1922, Belfast completed <code>OI</code> and was thus allocated the next available code, <code>XI</code>, while <code>YI</code> and then <code>ZI</code> were allocated to [[Dublin|Dublin City]]. After the partition, all codes with Z as the first letter (<code>ZA</code>, <code>ZB</code> etc.) were allocated in the Republic of Ireland, while all codes with Z as the second letter (<code>AZ</code>, <code>BZ</code> etc.) were allocated in Northern Ireland with the exception of County Mayo which had originally been allocated IZ. <code>IG</code> was allocated to Fermanagh in 2004. <code>VI</code> was allocated to City of Derry/Londonderry in 2023.

